AI Hiring Landscape 2024–2025

High Competition: A single AI job opening can receive ~250 applications, yet <3% of applicants get an interview. Your CV must immediately impress.

Rapid Scans: Recruiters often spend only ~7 seconds on an initial CV scan – first impressions count.

ATS Filters: ~90–98% of large companies use Applicant Tracking Systems to screen resumes. Only ~25% of resumes ever reach a human reader, usually due to formatting or missing keywords.

Implication: You need a targeted, concise, and well-formatted CV that highlights relevant AI skills and achievements upfront, to pass automated scans and grab human attention.



---

What Recruiters Look For in AI CVs

Proven Skills & Projects: Top AI employers prioritize demonstrated ability over just credentials. They value hands-on experience like internships, industry projects, impactful side projects, and open-source contributions. Show you’ve built or contributed to real AI solutions.

Impact & Results: Hiring managers seek “nuggets of gold” in your CV – clear evidence of how you made a difference. Metrics (accuracy improvements, speedups, revenue gains, user growth) catch their eye.

Relevant Tooling & Domain Experience: Showcase experience with tools and domains similar to the target role. DeepMind notes that having worked on similar projects and using relevant ML tools is highly valued.

Adaptability & Learning: AI evolves quickly. Highlight your ability to pick up new frameworks or research (e.g. mention if you implemented the latest model or kept pace with new techniques).

Communication & Teamwork: Soft skills matter even in highly technical AI roles. Employers want engineers who can collaborate and explain complex ideas clearly. Indicate teamwork (e.g. “collaborated with researchers on X”) and communication (e.g. “presented findings to stakeholders”).



