Early-Career vs. Experienced: Tailoring Your CV

For Early-Career Professionals (Entry-Level):

Emphasize Education: If you’re a new graduate or have <1-2 years of experience, place your Education section towards the top (after summary). Mention relevant courses (e.g. Machine Learning, Algorithms, Data Mining) and academic projects. Include academic honors or a strong GPA (if ~>3.5/4.0) to strengthen credibility.

Academic Projects = Experience: Treat substantial university projects, thesis, or capstones like work experience. Describe them with outcome-focused bullets. E.g., “Built a CNN to classify plant diseases with 92% accuracy as a final-year project.” This shows practical skill application.

Internships & Freelance: List internships, research assistantships, or freelance gigs in AI/ML as you would jobs. These count as real experience to employers. Even a 3-month internship at a startup or a Kaggle competition medal is valuable.

Personal Projects & Competitions: Showcase self-driven projects – a GitHub project, a Kaggle competition participation (especially if you ranked well), or a hackathon win in AI. These demonstrate passion and initiative. Include a link if the project is viewable online (GitHub repository, demo).

Skills & Certifications: As someone building experience, you can bolster your CV with certifications (e.g. “Google Data Analytics Certificate”) or relevant online courses. While not equal to experience, they show proactive learning.

Keep it Concise: For early careers, a 1-page resume is usually enough (and expected). You can briefly mention non-AI jobs or extracurriculars if they show transferable skills (leadership, programming practice, etc.), but focus on AI-related content.


For Experienced Professionals:

Lead with Experience: Your professional Experience should take center stage (put it before education). Focus on your last 5-10 years of roles that are relevant to AI. You might exclude very old or irrelevant jobs to keep the narrative tight.

Highlight Major Achievements: Use your CV to brag humbly – e.g., “Led a team of 5 engineers to deploy an NLP platform that processes 1M queries/day” or “Architected a data pipeline handling 2TB of data weekly, improving training speed by 3x.” These show leadership, scale, and impact in ways an early-career CV can’t.

Leadership & Mentoring: If you’ve mentored juniors, led projects, or managed stakeholders, weave that in. E.g., “Mentored 3 junior data scientists, improving team productivity” or “Project managed a cross-functional team (PMs, designers, engineers) to deliver an AI feature on time.” This signals you’re ready for senior responsibilities.

Industry Recognition: Briefly note any notable recognition: patent awards, invited talks, major conference presentations, or awards (e.g. “Winner of internal hackathon at Google”). Such accolades help differentiate you at the senior level.

Keep it Relevant & Updated: Remove or minimize outdated technologies or ancient programming languages unless still pertinent. Emphasize your continuous learning to stay at the cutting edge (e.g., mention if you recently upskilled in transformers or took a course in advanced ML to remain current). This shows you won’t be left behind in the fast-moving AI field.

Length: A 2-page CV is acceptable and often recommended for experienced candidates – it allows you to showcase a rich career. In fact, studies suggest recruiters are fine with two pages for seasoned professionals (77% of employers agree), and a two-page resume can be 2.9x more likely to land interviews for senior roles. Just ensure every line earns its place; no fluff.



