Personal Branding & Online Presence

Consistent Branding: Think of your CV as part of your personal brand as an AI professional. If you have a niche or passion (e.g. “AI in healthcare” or “Open-source enthusiast”), let that focus shine through in your CV by highlighting relevant projects. Maintain consistency in how you describe yourself across your CV, LinkedIn, and other profiles.

Portfolio & Links: In 2025, recruiters will often Google you or check your GitHub. Make it easy – include links to:

GitHub or Bitbucket: Especially if you have noteworthy code repositories. e.g., “GitHub: github.com/yourusername” in your contact info. Ensure your GitHub README and pinned projects reflect your best work.

LinkedIn: A must-have. Keep it aligned with your CV (same dates, titles, etc.) and add a bit more narrative there.

Personal Website/Blog: If you have one showcasing your projects or writing, include it. A website can host detailed case studies of your projects, which is great if a recruiter wants to dive deeper.

Kaggle or Stack Overflow (if strong): Only if you have a notable rank or contributions that you’re proud of (e.g., Kaggle competitions medals). These can reinforce your expertise.


Use Online Presence to Augment CV: Your CV is necessarily brief, but your online presence can provide depth. For example, a CV bullet might mention a project; your personal site could have a full article about it. Or your LinkedIn posts might show you engaging with the AI community. Employers do check for intellectual curiosity and passion.

Professional Social Media: If you’re active on Twitter (X) or relevant forums (Reddit ML threads, etc.) in a professional capacity, it’s optional to mention. Many AI researchers/engineers showcase their thoughts on Twitter or Medium. Just ensure anything you link is professional and up-to-date.

Personal Branding Elements: Some use a personal logo or consistent style across documents. This is optional – the content matters far more than fancy design – but a subtle, clean personal brand can make you memorable. For instance, using the same font/colors on your CV as on your website, or a personal tagline. Keep it very professional (this isn’t a graphic design portfolio; minimalism is usually best in tech resumes).



