Common Mistakes to Avoid

One-Size-Fits-All Resume: Sending the same generic CV to every job is a top mistake. AI roles can vary (research vs. engineering vs. data-focused). Always tailor your CV – adjust the summary, reorder skills, or emphasize different projects to match the specific job requirements.

Too Much or Too Little: Balance detail. A 5-page thesis of a resume will likely be tossed (or truncated by ATS), while a sparsely filled one-pager can look like lack of experience. Include enough detail to prove your impact, but avoid filler. Aim for that sweet spot (~500-600 words as per trends) unless you truly need a second page for relevant content.

No Quantifiable Achievements: Don’t just list duties or technical buzzwords without context. “Worked on machine learning models” is far less convincing than specifying what you achieved. Every role or project should have at least one measurable outcome or concrete result. Lack of this is a missed opportunity to impress.

Poor Proofreading: Typos, grammatical errors, or inconsistent formatting (like switching bullet styles or date formats) will signal lack of attention to detail. Given AI engineering often demands precision (in coding, data, etc.), a CV with mistakes can be a deal-breaker. Use spell-check and even AI grammar tools (Grammarly, etc.) to catch errors, but also manually proofread. And absolutely get your contact info right!

Unprofessional Presentation: This includes things like using an inappropriate email (e.g. coolhacker@hotmail), including irrelevant personal info or photos (unless regionally standard). Stick to professional content – no personal photos, no mentioning unrelated hobbies in depth (one line of interests is okay at the end, but optional).

Overusing Keywords / AI Jargon: While you should include relevant keywords, don’t keyword-stuff or use jargon out of context. Recruiters can tell when a resume is written for bots versus humans. Ensure the language flows naturally and each keyword is backed by an example or context in your experience.

Misrepresentation: It should go without saying, but honesty is critical. Don’t claim experience you don’t have. In the AI field, it’s easy to quiz someone on specifics. Exaggerations like “expert in all ML domains” or listing a dozen programming languages you barely touched will backfire. Highlight your strengths, but stay truthful about your level of expertise.

Ignoring ATS Basics: Fancy PDF designs with graphics, or resumes made purely in LaTeX with weird formatting, can sometimes choke an ATS. If you did use a creative template, double-check that the text is selectable and linear for parsing. Avoid white text “tricks” or other gimmicks – they can get your resume discarded.

Not Including Links or Evidence: In AI especially, showing your work can amplify your CV. If you mention a project, consider linking to the code or a write-up. If you say you have an open-source contribution, link to it. Lack of any evidence (when it’s easy to provide via links) might make your claims less credible. Just ensure any link you include is professional and accessible.


*(Remember: The goal is a CV that is clear, credible, and targeted. If you avoid these pitfalls, you’re far ahead of many applicants.)*


